  "worked_solution": "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have:\n\n$$\ng(f(x))=x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace everxy occurrence of $x$ with $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =3x+k \\\\ g(f(x)) & =3f(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $f(x)=\\frac{x}{3}+7$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =3f(x)+k \\\\  & =3(\\frac{x}{3}+7)+k \\\\  & =x+21+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we also know that $g(f(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+21+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}+21+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ 21+k & =0 \\\\ k & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$",
